## Why a new tool was unavoidable

Triage flagged that `tools/radsrv.py` is **PAP only, with no EAP**, so it cannot carry an
EAP-Message at all, and that the DUT's own local RADIUS server will not emit a Notification on
demand — which is correct, and the `radius-server local` CLI confirms it: the sub-mode offers
only `auth-mac-promiscuous / authentication / domain-style / group / nas / server / user`, and
`authentication ?` only `eapmd5 / eaptls / mac / peap`. Nothing emits a notification.

So **`tools/radeap.py`** was written for this: a RADIUS server that speaks EAP-Identity,
EAP-Notification and EAP-MD5, sends a **Message-Authenticator** on every reply (RFC 3579 requires
it on any RADIUS packet carrying an EAP-Message) and a **State** attribute so the NAS can
correlate the challenge round trips. `tools/radius.py` already had EAP-Message (79) and the
Message-Authenticator helper, so the new tool is a state machine on top of the existing codec,
not a second RADIUS implementation.

Its `--notify TEXT` flag is what makes the A/B pair below possible: **the same server, the same
users, the same supplicant, the same port — one flag different.**

