**STEP C verdict: PASS — 2/2 supplicants matched their declared expectation.** **Both** MACs
received the Notification with the identical 50-byte text and **both** answered it; then the one
with the correct password got `EAP-Success` at 1.05 s and the one with the wrong password got
`EAP-Failure` at 0.05 s. So the notification is relayed per supplicant, independently of the
outcome, and it rescues nobody.

### The three legs side by side

| leg | server `--notify` | EAPOL frames | Notification relayed? | outcome |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A (control) | no | 6 | **no** (server counter: sent 0) | EAP-Success, 1.03 s |
| B | yes | 8 | **yes**, text intact, response relayed back | EAP-Success, 1.04 s |
| C good credential | yes | — | **yes** | EAP-Success, 1.05 s |
| C bad credential | yes | — | **yes** | EAP-Failure, 0.05 s |

## The T33360 finding reproduced, independently, in a second case

Setting `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` for leg C **again silently rewrote
`dot1x control-direction both` to `in`**:

```
awplus#show running-config interface port1.0.48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction in            <- nobody typed this, in this case either
 auth host-mode multi-supplicant
```

`both` had been explicitly restored at the end of T33360 and verified there, so this is a clean
second observation of the same behaviour in a different case several hours later, not a leftover.
It is corroboration for the finding written up in
`tb277/x960/dot1x-2026-10-23T1304/T33360/work/run1.log`. Restored explicitly again here.

