## Verdict

The authored objective says `Not Support`. **It is wrong.** With a RADIUS server that actually
emits one, the DUT's 802.1X authenticator relays an EAP-Request/Notification to the supplicant
with its 50-byte text intact byte for byte, relays the supplicant's mandatory empty
EAP-Response/Notification back to the server, and completes the authentication normally
afterwards — proved by an A/B pair differing in exactly one flag (6 frames and no notification
versus 8 frames and a notification), with both ends of the exchange recorded, and by a paired leg
in which two supplicants both receive the notification while only the one with the right password
is admitted.

TPS row 189 is correct and the authored objective is not. The discrepancy is reported here
whichever way it was going to grade, as the dispatch required.

VERDICT: PASS -- EAP-Notification IS supported, contradicting the authored objective of `Not Support` and confirming TPS row 189 (`Yes`). The authenticator relays an EAP-Request/Notification from the RADIUS server to the supplicant with the text byte-for-byte intact (50 bytes in, 50 bytes out), relays the mandatory empty EAP-Response/Notification back (confirmed in the server's own log), and the authentication then proceeds normally; the control leg, identical but for one server flag, carries no notification at all (6 frames vs 8), and a bad credential in the same window is still refused with EAP-Failure.
